From 96c9bf74bf3d8875e568ee531a242f71f78a2e59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:17:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] tests: cover the CPU locality-domain split

tests/test-cpu-device-split.cpp (new CTest binary, registered alongside
test-barrier.cpp in the "cannot be built with dynamic loading" block):
builds a synthetic 2-domain sysfs fixture via GGML_CPU_LOCALITY_SYSFS_ROOT,
then asserts ggml_backend_dev_count()/get() report exactly 2 disjoint,
non-overlapping 4-core CPU devices and that both are independently
constructible via ggml_backend_dev_init(). Runs in its own process
(CTest gives every binary a fresh process) since locality-domain
detection is memoized once per process -- deliberately does not also
assert the real-hardware N=1 case in the same binary; that path is
covered by the live single-domain server smoke test noted in the
commit history and FLEET.md instead.

tools/server/tests/unit/test_cpu_split.py (new, modeled on
test_slot_save.py's ServerPreset/fixture pattern): three cases against
a real started llama-server -- --cpu-split off, --cpu-split auto
against the real (single-domain) host, and --cpu-split auto against a
mocked 2-domain GGML_CPU_LOCALITY_SYSFS_ROOT fixture -- each asserts a
normal /completion request still succeeds. tools/server/tests/utils.py
gained cpu_split and cpu_locality_sysfs_root fields on ServerProcess to
drive both from the test harness, following the existing per-flag
field convention (no_mmproj, sleep_idle_seconds, etc.).

All three pytest cases and the new CTest binary pass locally; existing
test-barrier and the full test-backend-ops -b CPU suite (op-correctness
regression, unaffected by this change's device-registry additions)
both still pass.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet
